### Page 7 of 22

```text
UDC-23-00016

February 6, 2023

One property owner (West side of Ridge Road between Charles and Cheyenne Streets) has indicated that
they may desire Mixed-use Business (MUB). Discussion is continuing with that property owner, and if they
request MUB zoning, staff will present separate rezoning request to the Planning Commission for review and
recommendation.

The lot on the southwest corner of Cheyenne Street and Wills Road presently under a County Site Plan
Review for establishment of a landscaping business that proposes development of an equipment storage
yard and equipment building with an office. Staff has been in communication with the County Planning Office
regarding this proposal and intends to recognize the approved site plan as a non-conforming use to allow
development to occur in compliance with the approval.  This will be a non-conforming use subject to the
nonconforming use provisions of the Unified Development Code (UDC § 1.3.6). in the event a nonconforming
use wished to change, expand, or intensify approvals may be granted by the Board of Adjustment through
Conditional Use approval pursuant to UDC § 1.3.6.c.1.

 ANALYSIS OF APPLICATION CONFORMANCE WITH REVIEW CRITERIA:

Should the Planning Commission wish to recommend approval of the Zoning Map Amendment, the
Commission must make the following findings required in UDC 2.2.1.d. A detailed analysis is below:

1) The application is in accordance with the development policies and physical patterns
identified in the Comprehensive Plan.  (UDC 2.2.1.d.1)

PlanCheyenne encourages growth within the Urban Service Boundary and the maximization of
existing infrastructure. This property is within the Urban Service Boundary and in an area with
existing infrastructure. All adjacent infrastructure (roads and utilities) is within and maintained
by the City.

PlanCheyenne encourages growth within the Urban Service Boundary and the maximization of
existing infrastructure. This property is within the Urban Service Boundary, in an area with
existing infrastructure. PlanCheyenne also encourages infill development (although it is not the
intent of the plan to change existing, stable neighborhoods). Zoning of this land concurrent with
a City-initiated annexation, removes future obstacles towards development tin the future.

The Future Land Use designation for this property is Mixed-use Commercial. Areas to the west
and south are also designated Mixed-use Commercial. According to comprehensive plan the
optimal City zone for the subject property’s future land use designation of Mixed-use
Commercial is MUB. However, CB zoning provides for commercial development and is generally
encouraged near activity centers.

Based on the policies and future land use map, CB Community Business zoning is appropriate at
this location.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 1.
```

### Page 8 of 22

```text
UDC-23-00016

February 6, 2023
2) The application is compatible with nearby property, specifically considering existing zoning,
the Comprehensive Plan, and any other official plans and policies created under the guidance
of that plan for these areas; (UDC 2.2.1.d.2)

These properties and surrounding properties are developed with a variety of uses with mixed-
use zoning designations (See Surrounding Land Use And Zoning section above). These properties
have developed with a variety of commercial uses within the County, some in accordance with
the present Count MU (Mixed Use) zoning and others likely prior to the establishment of County
MU zoning in the area. A few of the properties are underdeveloped and lend themselves to
future redevelopment.  Any redevelopment timeline would be initiated by the landowners on
their own timeline. Redevelopment of these properties would require adherence to City
development standards, including landscaping and buffer yards, which are intended to provide
compatibility with adjacent uses. The proposed CB zoning would be compatible with the
development patterns in the vicinity.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 2.

3) The suitability of the subject property for development according to the standards and uses of
the existing zoning district, compared to the suitability of the subject property for
development according to the standards and uses of the proposed zoning district (UDC
2.2.1.d.3)

The current zoning is a County zone and a City zone is required to develop within City limits. The
City zone most analogous to the current County MU zone is the City CB zone. The CB zone is
intended for more automobile-centric development. This location along two major
thoroughfares is more likely to lead to develop with commercial-style uses similar to those in
the surrounding area.

Neither the City CB or MUB (Mixed-use Business) zone districts permit outdoor storage as a
principal use, exisitng outdoor storage (active and entitled through a County Site Plan approval)
would be allowed to continue as nonconforming uses (UDC § 1.3.6).

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 3.

4) The ability of the subject property and surrounding vicinity to be developed according to the
Subdivision Standards in Article 4 considering the proposed zoning (UDC 2.2.1.d.4)

Article 4 of the Unified Development Code (UDC) provides standards for coordinating
development across different areas and over time. It addresses development patterns and
includes planning, design, and engineering standards for systems that coordinate development
across large areas, including open space, transportation, and utilities.

The subject properties are located on platted lands and surrounded by existing development.
Development of the subject properties could conform to majority of standards within Article 4
of the Unified Development Code.   In the event the requirements are impracticable or are
impossible, avenues for administrative waivers do exist.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 4.
```

### Page 9 of 22

```text
UDC-23-00016

February 6, 2023
5) The ability of the application to support the Intent and Applicability statements of the
proposed zoning district and achieve the Design Objectives of any specific standards. (UDC
2.2.1.d.5)

The CB zone is intended for larger commercial uses serving the community as a whole and is
applicable in activity centers. Internal circulation between adjacent sites is encouraged. The CB
zone applies commercial design standards. Higher intensity uses are appropriate near major
transportation corridors.

The proposed zone change for the subject property largely complies with review criterion 5.

6) The ability of all potential future development to meet all applicable standards for
development in the proposed zoning district, including other development options if any
specific development proposed in association with the application is not executed after
approval. (UDC 2.2.1.d.6)

There is no specified development proposed in concert with this rezoning. A variety of
commercial uses could develop at the location in accordance with applicable development
standards.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 6.

7) The review and recommendations of other reviewing bodies. (UDC 2.2.1.d.7)

No reviewing agencies provided recommendations regarding the proposed zone change.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 7.

8) Whether the application will harm the public health, safety, or general welfare. (UDC
2.2.1.d.8)

City staff is satisfied that this proposed zone change would not adversely affect the public
health, safety, or general welfare.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 8.

CONFORMANCE WITH APPLICABLE PLANS:

The project is located in an area covered by the following plans:
•
PlanCheyenne

Conformance with PlanCheyenne is covered by review criterion 1.

STAFF REVIEW:

Through the review process, there were no comments.
